<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      Whether ExactAlarmPermissionReceiver exists at all, on this device's platform version.

      The receiver listens for SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM_PERMISSION_STATE_CHANGED, an action that arrived
      in API 31. Its protection is a <protected-broadcast> entry in the platform's OWN manifest, and
      that entry is versioned with the platform image: an API 26-30 image cannot list an action that
      did not exist yet. Measured on the Galaxy S9 (API 29): `am broadcast` from uid 2000 reaches
      this receiver and returns result=0, while the same command against BOOT_COMPLETED is refused
      with a SecurityException. Same emitter, same channel, opposite outcomes.

      Delivering that broadcast STARTS THE PROCESS, so a guard inside onReceive cannot prevent the
      cost it is meant to prevent — MainApplication.onCreate has already opened SQLCipher and talked
      to the Keystore by then. Only non-existence closes it, and a component whose android:enabled
      resolves to false does not exist as far as PackageManager is concerned: no intent, explicit or
      not, can start it.

      false below API 31  — the action is unprotected there AND the app has nothing to do with it.
      true on API 31-32   — SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM is revocable on Android 12, so the grant-back signal
                            is real, and the platform now protects the broadcast.
      false on API 33+    — SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM is declared android:maxSdkVersion="32"; beyond it
                            USE_EXACT_ALARM takes over, and it cannot be revoked. Nothing to listen
                            for.

      i.e. the receiver exists for exactly the two API levels where it can do its job.
    -->
    <bool name="exact_alarm_receiver_enabled">false</bool>
</resources>
